     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe as written.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily adds up to **5,100 mg per day**. The usual maximum for adults is 2,550 mg per day for immediate-release and 2,000 mg per day for extended-release. This order is about twice the maximum, which raises the risk of lactic acidosis and serious stomach and bowel side effects. The amount looks like it may be a mix-up. 1700 mg per day split into two doses (850 mg twice daily) is common, and so is 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day). Either could be what was meant.\n\n**2. A prescriber needs to place the order.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders need to come from someone licensed to prescribe, such as the patient's attending physician, a resident or a nurse practitioner on the medicine service. They're accountable for the order and can check it against the patient's labs, blood sugar and other medications. Entering it on your behalf would get around that safeguard, even with good intentions.\n\n**What I'd suggest:** Ask the patient's treating team to confirm the intended dose and enter the order themselves. If you're passing along their request, they can also ask me directly and I'll help them enter it quickly once the dose is clarified."
      }
     ],
